  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a survey within a single German city, which may limit how well these specific perceptions generalize to different housing markets and cultural contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzed survey responses from residents living in buildings with and without green facades in Leipzig, Germany, using exploratory factor analysis and Bayesian linear regression. It found that residents with firsthand experience of green facades hold significantly more favorable views of them, while concerns about maintenance-related rent increases were primarily confined to those without direct exposure.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that direct, daily exposure to nature-based solutions can actively mitigate specific public anxieties like cost-driven displacement, shifting the focus of urban greening policy from general public relations to physical, experiential exposure."
    }
  },
